-- title:
--   Locality of integral subrings via marked Galois descent
-- statement:
--   Let $\Omega$ be a field, $R_0\le\Omega$ a subring, $F_0,\Lambda\le\Omega$ subfields and $\varphi\colon\Omega\to\Omega$ a ring homomorphism. Assume $R_0\subseteq F_0$; that every $x\in F_0$ admits $y,z\in R_0$ with $z\neq 0$ and $xz=y$ (so $F_0$ is a field of fractions of $R_0$); that $\varphi(F_0)\subseteq\Lambda$; and that there is a finite set $S\subseteq\Lambda$ each of whose elements is integral for the ring homomorphism $R_0\to\Omega$, $x\mapsto\varphi(x)$, with $\Lambda$ contained in the subfield generated by $\varphi(F_0)\cup S$. Let $T\le\Omega$ be a subring with $T\subseteq\Lambda$ and $T$ contained in the subring generated by $\varphi(R_0)\cup S$, and let $(G_i)_{i\in\iota}$ be a family of subsets of $T$. Call a subring $B'\subseteq\Lambda$ admissible if it contains $\varphi(R_0)$ and $T$ and all its elements are integral for $x\mapsto\varphi(x)$ on $R_0$. Three hypotheses are imposed: (covering) every maximal ideal of every admissible $B'$ contains some $G_i$; (uniqueness) two prime ideals of an admissible $B'$ containing the same $G_i$ are equal; (transport) for all $i,j$ there is a ring automorphism $\sigma$ of $\Lambda$ fixing every element of $\Lambda$ lying in $\varphi(F_0)$, such that for every subring $B'\subseteq\Lambda$ containing $T$ and stable under $\sigma$, every ideal of $B'$ containing $G_i$ also contains $\sigma(G_j)$. Then for every subring $B$ with $R_0\le B\le F_0$ all of whose elements are integral over $R_0$, any two maximal ideals $P_1,P_2$ of $B$ coincide.
--
--   This is a Galois-descent criterion for locality, transcribed into "subring currency": the conclusion says that any intermediate ring between $R_0$ and its fraction field which is integral over $R_0$ has at most one maximal ideal, deduced from a transitivity property of maximal ideals in the integral closure upstairs via the cited statement that $\mathrm{Frac}$-level integral closures are local when maximal ideals are permuted transitively by $\mathrm{Gal}(L/F)$. It is used in the study of the descended node ring of a modular curve, at the points where $j=0$ or $j=1728$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Subring_eq_of_isMaximal_of_marked_galois_descent.lean

